 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "BRUCE'S STONE. A great boulder above Loch Trool, remembering the spring of 1307, when Robert the Bruce's small band ambushed an English force by rolling rocks down the hillside. Gravity took a side."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "LOCHAN. A lochan is a small loch, and this one is about the size of a football pitch and as busy as the pitch on a Sunday."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "RED DEER RANGE. Red deer are Britain's largest wild land animal. The stags grow a fresh set of antlers every year and drop the old ones in spring, which is a better trick than most of us manage with a gym membership."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 41,
   "text": "GALLOWAY FOREST PARK. About three hundred square miles of forest, hill and loch, the biggest forest park in Britain. The Merrick, 843 m, is the highest hill in southern Scotland. The view is free."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "GALLOWAY DARK SKY PARK. In 2009 this became the first Dark Sky Park in the UK, which means the streetlights stay away and the stars come out in their thousands. Look up. No, further up. There."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 39,
   "plaque": "galloway_murrays_monument",
   "title": "Murray's Monument",
   "text": "Alexander Murray, a shepherd's son born in these hills in 1775, taught himself languages from borrowed books while minding sheep. He became Professor of Oriental Languages at Edinburgh University. An obelisk on the hillside above the Queen's Way remembers him."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "galloway_belted_galloways",
   "title": "Belted Galloways",
   "text": "The Belted Galloway is a hardy native breed from these parts, usually black, with a thick shaggy coat that shrugs off wind and rain and a broad white band round its middle. Locally they are Belties. They look like a cow wearing a cummerbund, and seem quite pleased about it."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 47,
   "plaque": "arts_burns_dumfries",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Robert Burns in Dumfries",
   "text": "Robert Burns, born at Alloway in Ayrshire on 25 January 1759, farmed at Ellisland and then moved to Dumfries in 1791 to work as an exciseman. There he wrote and gathered hundreds of songs for the Scots Musical Museum, among them 'Auld Lang Syne' and 'A Man's a Man for a' That'. He died in Dumfries on 21 July 1796, aged 37, and lies in the mausoleum at St Michael's. His friends held the first Burns Supper in 1801; the world holds one every 25 January. 'Should auld acquaintance be forgot' is the most-sung question on Earth, and the answer is no."
  }
 ],
